<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Setsen Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1580 CE – 1691 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The eastern of the three Khalkha khanates of Outer Mongolia, in the Kherlen valley, which submitted to the Qing with the rest of Khalkha in 1691.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Among the descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geresenje">Geresenje</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayan-khan">Dayan Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a>, the princes of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kherlen-river">Kherlen</a> river took the title Setsen Khan in the late 16th century, forming one of the three great divisions of Khalkha alongside the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tusheet-khanate">Tüsheet Khanate</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zasagt-khanate">Zasagt Khanate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its khans ruled herding peoples of the eastern steppe under a council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghisid-principle">Chinggisid</a> nobles. Being nearest to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, the khanate had early contact with the rising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sholoi-setsen-khan">Sholoi Setsen Khan</a> exchanged embassies and sent tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-jin-1616-1636">Later Jin</a> (1616-1636) and the early <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>, sometimes clashing with them over raids and border subjects. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug Buddhism</a> spread here as across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> ruler Galdan invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> in 1688, the Setsen Khan's people fled towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>. At the assembly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolon-nor">Dolon Nor</a> in 1691 the Khalkha princes accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing</a> rule, and the khanate became a Qing administrative league.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/setsen-khanate.jpg" alt="Setsen Khanate" width="320" height="161"><figcaption>Image: 瑞丽江的河水 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E8%BD%A6%E8%87%A3%E6%B1%97%E9%83%A8%E4%BD%8D%E7%BD%AE%E5%9B%BE1820.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1580 CE – 1691 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sholoi Setsen Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Khalkha Mongolian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Setsen Khan Aimag</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1636&amp;focus=setsen-khanate" aria-label="Setsen Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7147 565 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Setsen Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7817 927h0M7567 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7467 821h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7467 821h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Mongolia. <a href="/atlas?year=1636&amp;focus=setsen-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
